## The reuse problem
Matrix multiply $C = AB$ with $A$ of size $M \times K$ and $B$ of size $K \times N$ does $2MNK$ floating-point operations on only $MK + KN$ inputs. Every element of $A$ is used $N$ times and every element of $B$ $M$ times. The question is where that reuse happens. In the naive kernel, each thread computes one $C_{ij}$ by reading row $i$ of $A$ and column $j$ of $B$ straight from global memory:

```cpp
float acc = 0.f;
for (int k = 0; k < K; ++k) acc += A[row * K + k] * B[k * N + col];
C[row * N + col] = acc;
```

Each iteration does 2 FLOPs and loads 8 bytes: 0.25 FLOP/byte. On an A100 (19.5 TFLOPS FP32, about 2 TB/s), that intensity caps the kernel near 0.5 TFLOPS if every load went to HBM. Caches recover some of it, but the naive kernel typically reaches a few percent of cuBLAS.

## Tiled matmul
Split $C$ into $T \times T$ tiles, one per block of $T \times T$ threads. To compute its tile, the block walks along $K$ in steps of $T$; in each phase it cooperatively loads one $T \times T$ tile of $A$ and one of $B$ into shared memory, then every thread does $T$ multiply-adds from shared memory.

%%flow One phase of the tiled loop; the block repeats it K/T times.
Load A and B tiles (coalesced) -(__syncthreads)-> *Multiply from shared memory -(__syncthreads)-> Next phase
%%

```cpp
#define T 32
__global__ void matmul_tiled(const float* A, const float* B, float* C, int M, int N, int K) {
    __shared__ float As[T][T], Bs[T][T];
    int row = blockIdx.y * T + threadIdx.y, col = blockIdx.x * T + threadIdx.x;
    float acc = 0.f;
    for (int k0 = 0; k0 < K; k0 += T) {
        As[threadIdx.y][threadIdx.x] = (row < M && k0 + threadIdx.x < K) ? A[row * K + k0 + threadIdx.x] : 0.f;
        Bs[threadIdx.y][threadIdx.x] = (k0 + threadIdx.y < K && col < N) ? B[(k0 + threadIdx.y) * N + col] : 0.f;
        __syncthreads();                       // tile fully loaded
        for (int k = 0; k < T; ++k) acc += As[threadIdx.y][k] * Bs[k][threadIdx.x];
        __syncthreads();                       // done reading before overwrite
    }
    if (row < M && col < N) C[row * N + col] = acc;
}
```

Per phase, the block loads $2T^2$ floats ($8T^2$ bytes) and performs $2T^3$ FLOPs, so global intensity is $T/4$ FLOP/byte: 8 for $T = 32$, a 32x improvement. Both loads are coalesced, because `threadIdx.x` walks along a row of each tile. Out-of-range elements are filled with zeros so that matrices whose sizes are not multiples of $T$ work unchanged.

The two barriers are not optional. Remove the first and a fast warp reads tile entries that a slow warp has not yet written. Remove the second and a fast warp starts loading the next phase's tile over values a slow warp is still multiplying. Both bugs give wrong answers only sometimes, which makes them painful to find; `compute-sanitizer --tool racecheck` detects them. A barrier must also be reached by all threads of the block: never place `__syncthreads()` inside a branch that only some threads take.

## Bank conflicts
Shared memory is divided into 32 banks; successive 4-byte words go to successive banks. A warp's shared-memory access completes in one pass if its 32 threads hit 32 different banks, or the same word (broadcast). If $k$ threads hit different words in one bank, the access is replayed $k$ times.

| Access by a warp (float tile `s[32][32]`) | Banks touched | Cost
| Row: `s[r][tid]` | 32 distinct | 1 pass
| Same element: `s[r][k]` for all threads | 1 word, broadcast | 1 pass
| Column: `s[tid][c]` | all in bank `c` | 32 passes (32-way conflict)
| Column on padded `s[32][33]`: `s[tid][c]` | 32 distinct | 1 pass

In the tiled matmul above, a warp is one row of the block (fixed `threadIdx.y`, varying `threadIdx.x`). `As[threadIdx.y][k]` is the same word for all 32 threads, a broadcast; `Bs[k][threadIdx.x]` is a row access. No conflicts. The matrix transpose is where conflicts bite: read a tile row-wise, write it out column-wise, and the column read from shared memory is a 32-way conflict unless the tile is declared `[32][33]`. The extra column shifts each row by one bank.

! Example: how far tiling gets you
! On a 4096 x 4096 FP32 matmul, the naive kernel reaches roughly 1% of cuBLAS throughput on a recent GPU, and shared-memory tiling gets to roughly 10%. The rest comes from register tiling (each thread computing a 4 x 4 or 8 x 8 output block so values loaded from shared memory are reused in registers), wider vectorised loads, double buffering that overlaps the next tile's load with the current tile's math, and, for FP16/BF16, Tensor Core instructions. Simon Boehm's step-by-step write-up measures each stage on a real GPU.

## Limits and trade-offs
Shared memory per block defaults to 48 KB for static arrays; larger allocations need dynamic shared memory and an explicit opt-in attribute. Every byte a block uses reduces how many blocks fit on an SM, which lowers occupancy (31.07). Two 32 x 32 FP32 tiles use 8 KB, small. A double-buffered 128 x 32 BF16 tiling uses much more and is designed with occupancy in mind.

> Takeaway: tile so that each byte fetched from global memory is reused many times from shared memory, fence each phase with two barriers, and lay out shared arrays so a warp never hits one bank with different addresses.

## When reductions are not enough
A reduction combines everything into one value along a fixed tree. Many kernels instead scatter updates to data-dependent locations: histograms (which bin?), gradient accumulation for embedding tables (which rows were looked up?), stream compaction with a shared output counter, MoE token counts per expert. Two threads may target the same location at the same time, and a plain `bins[b] += 1` then loses updates: both threads read 7, both write 8. Atomics fix this by making the read-modify-write indivisible.

```cpp
__global__ void hist_naive(const unsigned char* x, unsigned int* bins, int n) {
    for (int i = blockIdx.x * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x; i < n; i += gridDim.x * blockDim.x)
        atomicAdd(&bins[x[i]], 1u);
}
```

Supported operations include add, sub, min, max, exchange, and/or/xor and compare-and-swap, on 32- and 64-bit integers, `float`, `double` (compute capability 6.0 and up) and `__half2`. Anything else, such as a float max with an index or a custom combine, is built from `atomicCAS` in a retry loop: read the old word, compute the new one, swap only if the word has not changed, otherwise retry. Packing (value, index) into one 64-bit word lets a single CAS update both atomically.

## Contention
Global atomics are executed in the L2 cache. Updates to distinct addresses proceed in parallel at high throughput; updates to the same address are serialised. For a 256-bin histogram of 8-bit pixels, throughput depends on the image: natural photos spread across bins, while a mostly-black frame sends almost every update to bin 0.

%%flow Privatised histogram: most updates hit fast per-block shared memory; global memory sees one atomic per bin per block.
Input pixels -(shared atomicAdd)-> *Per-block bins in shared memory -(__syncthreads)-> Merge -(one global atomic per bin)-> Global bins
%%

## Privatisation
Give each block a private histogram in shared memory, initialise it to zero, accumulate with shared-memory atomics, synchronise, then add each non-zero private bin to the global histogram.

```cpp
__global__ void hist_private(const unsigned char* x, unsigned int* bins, int n) {
    __shared__ unsigned int local[256];
    for (int b = threadIdx.x; b < 256; b += blockDim.x) local[b] = 0;
    __syncthreads();
    for (int i = blockIdx.x * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x; i < n; i += gridDim.x * blockDim.x)
        atomicAdd(&local[x[i]], 1u);               // on-chip, per SM
    __syncthreads();
    for (int b = threadIdx.x; b < 256; b += blockDim.x)
        if (local[b]) atomicAdd(&bins[b], local[b]);
}
```

Shared-memory atomics have been native hardware operations since the Maxwell generation, so they are much cheaper than global ones, and contention is now limited to the threads of one block. Further steps when contention stays high: per-warp sub-histograms (several copies per block, merged at the end), having each thread pre-aggregate runs of identical values in registers before issuing an atomic, and launching only enough blocks to fill the GPU so the merge cost stays small.

| Strategy | Global atomics for $n = 10^8$, 256 bins, 1024 blocks | Worst-case contention
| Naive global | $10^8$ | all threads on one address
| Per-block shared bins | at most $1024 \times 256 = 262{,}144$ | threads of one block on one shared address
| Per-warp shared bins | same global count | 32 lanes on one address
| Warp-aggregated | up to 32x fewer than naive | one atomic per distinct address per warp

## Warp-aggregated atomics
When many lanes of a warp target the same address, as when appending to an output buffer through one shared counter, it is wasteful to issue 32 atomics. With `__ballot_sync` (or `__match_any_sync` for per-address groups) the lanes find out how many of them want to update, a leader lane does one `atomicAdd` of that count, broadcasts the returned base offset with a shuffle, and each lane computes its own slot from its rank among the active lanes. Recent compilers do this automatically for the simple case of a uniform address, but the pattern is worth knowing for filtering and compaction kernels.

## Determinism
Integer atomics give the same final value whatever the order. Floating-point atomics do not, because $(a + b) + c \ne a + (b + c)$ in floating point and the order of arrival varies run to run. This is why PyTorch's reproducibility notes list CUDA `index_add_` and `scatter_add_` among operations that are non-deterministic by default; with `torch.use_deterministic_algorithms(True)` such operations either switch to slower deterministic implementations (typically sort-then-reduce) or raise an error. If a test needs bitwise reproducibility, replace float atomics with a reduction in a fixed order.

! Example: contention in numbers
! Take a 256-bin histogram over 100 million bytes. On random bytes the naive kernel spreads its atomics over 256 addresses; on an all-zero input every one of the $10^8$ updates queues on a single L2 address, and the same kernel can slow down by an order of magnitude or more. The privatised version stays close to bandwidth-bound for random data and degrades far less on constant data, because the queue now lives in each SM's shared memory and the global merge is only 262,144 atomics.

> Takeaway: use atomics for scattered, data-dependent updates, expect throughput to depend on how many threads collide, and privatise into shared memory (or aggregate per warp) so that global memory sees a few atomics per block rather than one per element.
